# The parity allowlist. ONE file, in kit, for the whole fleet.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#
# A service adopts kit by COPYING. Every artefact in `tests/artifacts.json` is
# meant to be byte-identical to what kit ships, and `tests/staleness.py
# --scope templates` says so when it is. When it is not, this file is where the
# reason lives.
#
# Without it, `diverged` and `absent` are both permanently red and the fleet
# learns to ignore them — which is the same shape as the failure this repository
# exists to prevent, one directory over: a check that reports green precisely
# when there is something to report.
#
# WHY ONE FILE HERE AND NOT ONE PER SERVICE REPO
#
# The aggregate is the actual problem. Ten files each holding one line looks
# like bookkeeping and reads like none, and nobody sums them. Same argument as
# `templates/tier/skip-allowlist`, and for the same reason: a drifting copy of a
# short list is a list nobody reads.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR HYGIENE RULES
#
# These are `templates/tier/skip-allowlist`'s four rules, unchanged and in the
# same words. kit does not have two dialects of "record why", because two
# dialects is how one of them goes stale.
#
# 1. EVERY ENTRY NAMES A REASON.
#    Without one, "it is fine" becomes the reason for everything, and a reason
#    that means nothing cannot be reviewed, expired, or argued with.
#
# 2. EVERY ENTRY NAMES AN OWNER.
#    A divergence nobody owns is a divergence nobody will ever bring back into
#    line. The owner is a person or a team, not a distribution list.
#
# 3. EVERY ENTRY HAS AN EXPIRY (until) AND A since DATE.
#    `since` dates the decision; `until` is the ratchet. THE GATE READS THE
#    CLOCK: an entry whose `until` has passed is a FAILURE on the day it passes.
#    This file is expected to go red roughly four times a year, and each time the
#    correct response is to re-copy the artefact and delete the entry, or to move
#    the date with a NEW reason. A gate that has never gone red is a report.
#
# 4. AN ENTRY THAT MATCHES NOTHING IS A FAILURE.
#    Borrowed from ESLint's `reportUnusedDisableDirectives`, which reports a
#    disable comment that no longer suppresses anything. An entry for an
#    artefact that is now byte-identical to kit excuses nothing.
#
#    This ledger is checked in the OTHER direction too, and the tier allowlist
#    is not: an UNPINNED divergence or absence is a failure as well, because
#    "nobody wrote down why" is a fact about the fleet, not an exemption. Both
#    directions are needed, and they are the same rule seen from two ends. Rule
#    4 alone would let the file empty itself silently; the unpinned check alone
#    would let it grow without ever expiring.
#
#    THE TOTAL IS PRINTED ON PASS. Every entry individually looks justified. The
#    aggregate is the problem, and an aggregate nobody is shown is an aggregate
#    nobody watches — and a green run is where a growing list is least likely
#    to be noticed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
#
#   <verb> <repo> <artefact-id> reason="…" owner=… since=YYYY-MM-DD until=YYYY-MM-DD
#
# `diverged`  the service holds the file and it is not kit's bytes
# `absent`    kit ships it and the service holds nothing at that path
# `unknown`   kit cannot say what the service would have copied — the service
#             declares no `language`, so the source to compare against is
#             unresolvable. It is a finding and not a shrug, so it needs a pin
#             like the other two; recording it as `absent` would put a false
#             statement in this file to record a real fact.
#
# The verb is CHECKED AGAINST WHAT WAS MEASURED, not trusted. An entry that says
# `absent` for a file that is present and byte-identical is a dead entry, and
# one that says `diverged` for a file that is simply not there is a wrong one;
# both are failures, in the two different ways an allowlist rots.
#
# <artefact-id> is the `id` from `tests/artifacts.json`, verbatim. A `#` starts a
# comment. Blank lines are ignored. One entry per line, and the line must be ONE
# line: a wrapped reason is two entries, one of which is a parse error.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ENTRY COUNT IS A MEASUREMENT, NOT A LEDGER TO SHRINK
#
# Read the total the way you read the total in the tier allowlist: the number is
# the point. A small number here is achieved by making services match kit, NOT
# by deleting entries — and deleting an entry to make the count go down is now a
# hard failure of its own, which is the only reason this paragraph can be true.
# It is a claim, so it is enforced: the `parity-allowlist` check in
# `tests/validate.sh` reports the count on PASS as well as on FAIL.
#
# Everything below was written by MEASURING the fleet with the reporter, not by
# assuming. The comments say what the measurement found, and where the reason is
# "nobody has said", that is what it says.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

